WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.360
Tools turn an agent's output into access or action, so every tool boundary should be treated as a security boundary.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:14.820
An agent may be able to search records, send messages, update tickets, run workflows, modify files, or call administrative APIs.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.480
The model can propose an action, but trusted application logic should decide whether that action is allowed.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:25.100
Least privilege means giving the agent only the access needed for an approved task.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:30.840
Permissions can be limited by user, resource, action, environment, time, and business context.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:35.860
A support agent that summarizes a ticket may need read access but not permission to close it.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:40.480
An agent that drafts a message may not need authority to send it without review.

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:46.780
Separate capabilities such as read, draft, recommend, approve, and execute.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.240
Avoid combining them into one powerful tool simply because that is convenient.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:01:01.940
Use task-specific credentials where practical, limit token lifetime, protect secrets outside prompts, validate tool parameters, and apply rate or transaction limits.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.480
The tool service should recheck authorization instead of trusting the model's explanation.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:13.100
Broad standing access increases the impact of mistakes, misleading input, and compromised dependencies.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.200
Scoped tools and approval gates reduce that impact.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.140
The goal is not to prevent agents from being useful.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:25.600
It is to make useful actions explicit, bounded, observable, and reversible whenever the workflow allows it.
